WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
You ask AI for a plan,

2
00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.320
then ask it to review its own

3
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.420
work.

4
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.780
It approves it.

5
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:06.980
Every time.

6
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.640
The model that wrote the plan,

7
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.320
especially in the same session,

8
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.520
tends to think everything is fine.

9
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.460
I’m Nei Maldaner,

10
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.680
and the solution is simple:

11
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.580
one plans, the other critiques.

12
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.960
Claude and Codex complement each other when

13
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.180
each handles one part and the other

14
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.020
checks the actual artifact.

15
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:25.900
In this video,

16
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.540
I’ll show you how to use them

17
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.400
together and everything INEMA already has ready:

18
00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.500
courses, kits, and tools,

19
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.440
all

20
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:36.140
open.

21
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.940
The foundation is Mark Kashef’s Use Both

22
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.920
kit, with an open license.

23
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.340
It organizes work with both tools across

24
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.060
six levels, with

25
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.200
ready-to-use prompts and two small skills,

26
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.320
handoff and prime,

27
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.640
so you can switch assistants without explaining

28
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:51.580
the project again.

29
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.180
And one rule applies at every level:

30
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.520
the second assistant gets the same brief

31
00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.200
and the real artifact.

32
00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.640
The file, the diff,

33
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.440
the image.

34
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.460
Never a vague summary of what the

35
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.900
first one did.

36
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.960
Level one: let the two discuss.

37
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.920
One writes the plan,

38
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.940
the other critiques the same file,

39
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.480
read-only, with evidence and severity.

40
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.280
At most two rounds,

41
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.600
because two AIs agreeing isn’t proof.

42
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.300
Level two, optional:

43
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.120
generate images, only if there’s an image

44
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:23.940
tool in the session.

45
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.360
Done means the real file is open

46
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.520
at its intended size.

47
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.540
Level three: split building and reviewing.

48
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.300
One builds on a branch,

49
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.340
the other reviews the diff against the

50
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.180
brief and tests.

51
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.720
The tests and you decide what goes

52
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:40.120
in.

53
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.210
Level

54
00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.420
four: supervised computer use.

55
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.440
The assistant prepares an action in an

56
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.340
interface and stops before submitting it.

57
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.460
Passwords, payments, and important submissions stay with

58
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:51.640
you.

59
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.840
Level

60
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.020
five: a goal with a stopping condition.

61
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.940
Define observable success,

62
00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.480
which tests to run,

63
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.340
what output to expect,

64
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.800
and limits on files,

65
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.200
rounds, and spend.

66
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.160
Asking it to stop in two hours

67
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.380
is a request,

68
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.620
not a limit.

69
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.600
Level six: handoff and prime.

70
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.080
Handoff saves the state in a portable

71
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.620
file, and prime makes the next assistant

72
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.540
read and verify that state before continuing.

73
00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.580
In practice,

74
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:22.880
who does what?

75
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.660
For planning, Claude writes and Codex critiques

76
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.620
the assumptions until there’s a plan with

77
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.840
scope and acceptance tests.

78
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.500
To build a feature,

79
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.040
Claude builds and Codex reviews the diff.

80
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.340
For a tough bug,

81
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.600
use Codex with a bounded goal and

82
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:38.560
a human checkpoint.

83
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.940
To review a document,

84
00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.820
either one can write and the other

85
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.460
can check the facts.

86
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.380
To switch sessions or models,

87
00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:46.980
use handoff and prime.

88
00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.160
One note: these are editorial choices,

89
00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.660
not a measured ranking.

90
00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.080
Test them and stick with the route

91
00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.600
that gives you the best evidence.

92
00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.500
Mark Kashef’s video shows three ways to

93
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.120
connect the two.

94
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.880
The official Codex plugin for Claude Code.

95
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.580
One CLI calling the other,

96
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.820
with the response saved to a Markdown

97
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:08.800
file:

98
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.640
codex exec in read-only mode,

99
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.520
or claude minus p the other way

100
00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:13.940
around.

101
00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.260
And a pull request reviewed by the

102
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.160
other model.

103
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.480
In the author’s view,

104
00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.140
Claude is optimistic and good at planning,

105
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.260
while Codex is pessimistic and good at

106
00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.100
finding flaws.

107
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.820
One warning:

108
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.940
Codex’s goal command pursues the goal exhaustively,

109
00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.130
taking

110
00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.520
three to five times more time and

111
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.320
tokens.

112
00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:36.700
Set a real limit:

113
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.700
a timeout, a budget,

114
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.510
and a number of rounds.

115
00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.480
INEMA’s courses and projects about these two

116
00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.040
tools aren’t

117
00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.400
separate pieces.

118
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.180
They form a path.

119
00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.040
First, understand them with the Claude para

120
00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.160
Codex course, Codex Básico,

121
00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.340
Master Codex, and Codex Cheat Sheet.

122
00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.580
Then migrate or stay agnostic with the

123
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.860
agente claude codex kit.

124
00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.900
Next, use both together with Use Both.

125
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:05.820
Finally,

126
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.880
automate the debate with claudex.

127
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.040
The common thread is the project’s Markdown

128
00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.200
files: AGENTS dot md,

129
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:13.600
tasks, and handoffs,

130
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.160
which any runtime can read.

131
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.520
So what’s the difference between claudex and

132
00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:19.480
Use Both?

133
00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.340
claudex is software,

134
00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.060
a Claude Code plugin:

135
00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.540
Claude writes PLAN dot md,

136
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.900
Codex critiques it from three angles in

137
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.200
a loop, until it approves or reaches

138
00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:31.840
the number of rounds you set.

139
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.180
It has status and rollback.

140
00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.700
Use Both is a method:

141
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.380
a guide, prompts,

142
00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.540
templates, and the two skills,

143
00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.820
covering all six workflows.

144
00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.860
They complement each other.

145
00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.120
For a big plan,

146
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.070
use

147
00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:46.460
claudex.

148
00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.560
For everything else—reviewing a diff,

149
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.300
a goal with a stopping condition,

150
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.880
switching sessions—use Use Both.

151
00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.160
A real example.

152
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.133
At

153
00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:58.120
INEMA, Claude Code calls Codex in non-interactive

154
00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.560
mode and asks its image generator for

155
00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.260
a PNG file in a folder.

156
00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.860
That’s how catalog covers,

157
00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.240
guide banners with correct Portuguese text,

158
00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.780
and course scenes are made.

159
00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.100
If Codex fails,

160
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.420
it falls back automatically to local FLUX.2

161
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:13.040
klein.

162
00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.580
We took three habits from the kit:

163
00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.480
check the tool and cost before generating,

164
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.980
inspect the image at its real size,

165
00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.120
and save sibling versions instead of overwriting.

166
00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.700
And here’s everything available to you,

167
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.000
all open.

168
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.450
Six courses:

169
00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:30.820
Claude para

170
00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:32.660
Codex, in Portuguese,

171
00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:33.700
English,

172
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.380
and Spanish;

173
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.920
Codex Básico, the complete Codex CLI course;

174
00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:42.340
Master Codex, the SaaS factory with agents;

175
00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.000
iClaudeX, planning with Claude and Codex in

176
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.210
the terminal;

177
00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.460
MakeClaudeX, from prompt to production plugin;

178
00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:50.660
and

179
00:05:50.971 --> 00:05:53.346
DeepClaudeX, multi model,

180
00:05:53.346 --> 00:05:55.720
seventy, twenty, ten.

181
00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.040
Plus the kits:

182
00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:57.947
agente

183
00:05:57.947 --> 00:06:00.380
claude codex, to migrate or stay agnostic;

184
00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.900
Use Both, with the six levels;

185
00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.340
the claudex CLI;

186
00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.880
and Codex Cheat Sheet,

187
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:06.940
the quick reference.

188
00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.560
Everything has a GitHub repository.

189
00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.360
Where should you start?

190
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:12.760
Choose a small task.

191
00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.200
Write the brief and acceptance

192
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.160
criteria.

193
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.740
Ask one to make the plan and

194
00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:19.940
the other to critique it,

195
00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.220
using the same file,

196
00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.320
for at most two rounds.

197
00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.920
Before ending the session,

198
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.000
do the handoff.

199
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.070
You’ll find all this in the Codex

200
00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.540
plus Claude section at eventos

201
00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:31.600
dot inema dot pro,

202
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.600
slash

203
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.760
codex claude, in Portuguese,

204
00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.500
English, and Spanish.

205
00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.520
Open content from INEMA.CLUB.
